WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:02.804
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.710
- What's up guys, Michael
here to talk about

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.543
the high school show

00:00:07.543 --> 00:00:09.860
that has parents confiscating body glitter

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.530
all over the country.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.340
That's right, "Euphoria."

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.380
It provokes vivid reactions.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.380
Whether people are crying
over Rue's latest relapse,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.090
squirming at Nate's improbable awfulness

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.857
or counting onscreen dongs like Pokemon.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.880
"Euphoria" has garnered plenty of praise

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.480
for its expressive
cinematography and raw treatment

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.410
of contemporary high school life.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.540
But not without controversy.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.960
Some say it glorifies teen drug use

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.600
and other naughty behaviors.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.260
Others argue that it puts the audience

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.600
through the emotional ringer
without substantial pay off.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.710
But whether you think this
is prestige television

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.830
at its best or trauma porn
at its most shameless,

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.150
chances are you probably
agree that watching the show

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.860
feels something like getting
kicked in the stomach

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.720
by a runaway donkey in a crop top.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.350
Now it's not like the
show's various themes

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.270
from addiction to queerness
can't be found elsewhere

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.103
on television,

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:02.570
but on "Euphoria" everything
and painful trauma

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.870
to the most minor adolescent and dignity,

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.400
viscerally hurts.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.233
- Are you making fun of me

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.400
or did you actually think I
was auditioning for "Oklahoma?"

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.410
- [Narrator] So what exactly
makes this show so gutting?

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.190
Let's explore in this Wisecrack Edition

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.230
on "Euphoria, Empathy is Work"

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.967
and of course, spoilers
ahead for "Euphoria."

00:01:20.825 --> 00:01:23.580
When we think about this
standout moments on "Euphoria"

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.100
they are typically the
ones that are most painful

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.060
take for example, Cassie's anxiety driven

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.060
borderline violent grooming
behaviors to attract Nate.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.640
- I have never, ever been happier.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.050
- [Narrator] Or Rue bailing
out of her mom's car

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.960
and going on the lam to
avoid going back to rehab

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.600
as her sister Gia screams in panic.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.820
To understand why scenes

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.230
like these are so stressful to endure,

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.520
we need to bring in
the concept of empathy.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.960
I.e. the ability to understand

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.610
and share the feelings of another.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.600
While philosophers had
kicked this idea around

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.420
for centuries, there wasn't a term

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.470
for the concept until German
philosopher and art historian,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:03.360
Robert Vischer, used the
word Einfühlung in 1873,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.320
translated to empathy.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.240
It would enter the popular consciousness

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.170
around the beginning of the 20th century.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.900
And this way empathy like
Jules' glittery makeup

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.030
has a distinctly modern flare.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.790
Scholar Jeremy Rifkin,

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:17.530
attribute to the increasing importance

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.750
of empathy, to the rise
of modern psychology

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.920
which encouraged people
to delve more deeply

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.880
into their own internal world.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.320
Thus, complicating their self-perceptions.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.710
As human beings began thinking more deeply

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.650
and in a more nuanced way about
their feelings and thoughts,

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.480
they also started thinking
about those feelings

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.690
and thoughts in relation to
other people's inner worlds.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.900
As Rifkin explains, "It wasn't

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.140
until human beings were developed
enough in human selfhood

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.470
that they were able to recognize
the existence of empathy."

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.924
That is to say properly
interpreting what's going on

00:02:47.924 --> 00:02:50.900
for Rue when she jumps
out of that car, first

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.540
requires you to understand
your own potentially

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.180
self destructive impulses

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.550
and how those impulses might
be perceived by other people.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.350
As psychologist and philosopher,
Theodor Lipps put it,

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.460
Einfühlung is the process of tapping

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.410
into the emotional subjectivity
of another person's psyche

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.450
to the point where you can't
tell where your feelings end

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.820
and theirs begin,

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.060
like falling into a Jules,
Rue, Elliot cuddle puddle.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.930
It requires connecting to someone else

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.440
on a deeper level than we usually do.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:19.470
But at the same time,

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.160
the concept of empathy took off,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.030
modern society was encouraging
self-contained individuality.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.180
As a result, empathy weirdly
became more difficult.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.030
Just because humans had
discovered what it meant

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.000
to feel other people's subjectivity,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.530
it didn't mean we were
good at practicing it.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.700
Indeed studies have shown

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.780
that human beings still have
a tendency to see others,

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.120
especially say people
struggling with addiction

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.650
or overtly sexualized women
as simple abstractions

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.210
rather than full complex human beings.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.940
We see this behavior plenty in "Euphoria."

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:53.280
Rue is commonly dismissed
as being just a junkie.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.770
- In all fairness, last summer

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.190
there were like three weeks
where I thought you were dead.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.070
- [Narrator] Or like when
Cassie's potential hookup cruelly

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.150
tells her that...

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.600
- Any guy who says he's interested

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.080
in you beyond just (beep)
you is full of (beep)

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.930
- Here, both women are being seen

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.130
as simple abstractions

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:12.060
based on one aspect of
their being their drug use

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.260
or their sexuality.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.170
True empathy in contrast
requires seeing people

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.290
as more than the sum of their parts

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.580
and attempting to connect
to their subjectivity.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.517
As writer Elie Weisel put it,

00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:25.430
"We must not see any
person as an abstraction.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.700
Instead, we must see in
every person, a universe,

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.760
with its own secrets,
with its own treasures

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.193
with its own sources of anguish

00:04:32.193 --> 00:04:34.350
and with some measure of triumph."

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.750
Perhaps unsurprisingly,
due to their ability

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.270
to viscerally render
characters in her worlds, film

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.640
and later, television
have long been considered

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.360
useful mechanisms for eliciting empathy.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.587
As film critic, Roger Ebert once said,

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:50.090
"For me, the movies are like a machine

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.400
that generates empathy."

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.200
Now there's one good reason

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.700
any successful TV show
needs to elicit empathy.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.793
People are more likely to keep
coming back week after week

00:04:58.793 --> 00:05:00.910
if they actually give a (beep)

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.720
About the characters on screen.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.570
But "Euphoria" from our view is different.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.940
Here, empathy isn't merely a mechanism

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.540
to get people to stick around.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.927
It's the entire point of the show.

00:05:11.927 --> 00:05:14.221
"Euphoria", most literally spells this out

00:05:14.221 --> 00:05:16.190
when Rue's narcotics anonymous sponsor

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.970
talks about the way society
reduces people struggling

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.750
with addiction to abstractions.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.380
- We're too busy running around

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.330
judging everybody's
intentions and motivations

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.350
as if we have some insight
into the human soul,

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.100
you did this so that
must mean you're that,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:28.933
just gimme a break

00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:29.766
- [Narrator] But more subtly.

00:05:29.766 --> 00:05:32.280
The intention of empathy
is telegraphed throughout

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.280
the show's visual and narrative devices.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.210
Take the episodes which feature
Rue's voiceover narration.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.043
- She just knew

00:05:38.043 --> 00:05:41.970
that she had something
special, something intangible.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.130
- [Narrator] Now, the pilot
starts somewhat conventionally

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.580
with Rue narrating her own life story.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.680
- [Rue] I was once happy,
content, sloshing around

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.750
in my own private primordial pool.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.700
- [Narrator] Though, notably
it's evoking memories

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.934
she couldn't possibly have like being born

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:58.220
and what was on TV when she popped out.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.860
This gives it a surreal vibe.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.290
And as the series progresses

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.840
that omniscience becomes more
uncanny as even the most minor

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.820
of characters are given a few
minutes of Rue's narration.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.893
- [Rue] Or they could just sit in silence.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.160
They were that close, that comfortable.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.590
- [Narrator] These segments
are divulged from a close

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.780
third perspective, meaning it's presented

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.670
in the third person, but skews closely

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.880
to the character's actual lived experience

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.090
rather than remaining entirely objective

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:27.200
like say a news reporter would.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.840
Importantly for the most part,

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.580
Rue is narrating aspects
of these characters' lives

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.000
and inner worlds that she
couldn't possibly have access to.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.320
This creates a surreal sense
of almost uber-empathy.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.220
It also complicates the ways
we think about each character.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.410
It forces us to understand why each teen

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.160
or adult makes some unsavory choices

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.570
and to reconsider characters
whom we might normally

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.650
and reflexively judge or dismiss.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.250
What the show seems to be saying

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.430
about high school and
about life in general

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.610
is every person has (beep) going on

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.520
that you couldn't simply from looking

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.750
at them or talking to them,
such is the power of empathy.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:04.015
It's also impossible to talk
about Euphoria's production

00:07:04.015 --> 00:07:07.540
of empathy without discussing
its highly stylized

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:08.373
look and feel.

00:07:08.373 --> 00:07:10.180
First off, "Euphoria" traffic's heavily

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.860
in one shots, especially intense closeups,

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.900
which until very recently were
rarely seen on television.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.870
And still don't typically
show up on soapy teen shows.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:19.900
The rise of the closeup

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.070
was a really important
transformation in film history.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.260
Early film mostly used wide shots

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.130
as if you were watching a play,

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.610
but in a 1912 film called "Friends,"

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.270
we saw what film director Paul Schrader

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.170
calls the first true closeup.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.490
One of actress, Mary Pickford's face.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:36.970
This suddenly gave us access

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.000
to far more information of
about her emotional condition.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.360
It's perhaps no wonder then

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.440
that Mary Pickford became
Hollywood's true first celebrity.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.731
While actors and actresses had
previously gone uncredited,

00:07:47.731 --> 00:07:50.850
the rise of closeups made
audiences start to relate

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.980
to the performers as
people and thus love them

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.660
as much as you love Tomdaya.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.810
Euphoria's closeups are
often slow and searching,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.170
typically featuring a
face or part of a face

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:02.880
over a blurred out background.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.730
This gives viewers nothing
but that actor to engage with,

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.630
putting us as close to understanding

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:10.400
what the character feels
as visually possible,

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.440
but when it's not making you wanna barf

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.557
in the hot tub with Cassie,

00:08:13.557 --> 00:08:15.350
"Euphoria" is also using the kinds

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.250
of high octane camera movements

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.460
you're more accustomed to
seeing in action films.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.130
While being highly cinematic,

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:23.750
such movements also pump up our emotions.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.590
The camera might dive into a
pool to express the exuberance

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.380
of a drunken house
party, or it might slowly

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.250
dolly back to emphasize just
how alone a character feels.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.030
It might defy gravity
to help you get a sense

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.310
of what drugs feel like.

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:39.400
Or of course it might run
uninterrupted through a food cart,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.210
around the ground, the
carnival, and ultimately

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.210
up to a Ferris wheel showing
the personal intrigues

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.830
and heartaches of each
character along the way.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.170
The sequence in particular,

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.320
reinforces one of the show's key themes.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.050
The characters are all in
the same place physically.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.460
Sure, but they're each in a
complicated emotional universe

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.780
of their own, such as the
unadulterated roller coaster

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.220
of teenage hormones.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.820
And some of those roller coaster rides

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.610
are relentlessly long.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.670
It's pretty well accepted

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:07.990
that lengthy shots can be super effective

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.360
in building tension, more
so than quick cutaways.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.090
That's why the lingering shot in, say,

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.890
every slasher movie when you're waiting

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.590
for the killer to strike
is so much scarier

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.390
than the actual slashing itself.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.170
Our instinct is to look away

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:22.530
in search of some emotional relief

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.817
by lingering almost unbearably
long in many of its shots,

00:09:25.817 --> 00:09:27.662
"Euphoria" doesn't give us that relief.

00:09:27.662 --> 00:09:29.710
Instead, like a slasher film,

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:32.370
it makes us sit in the
moment as the tension builds.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.490
But instead of a scary guy with a knife,

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:35.730
the thing we are are confronting

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.297
is the high intensity emotions
being felt by the characters.

00:09:39.297 --> 00:09:42.130
"Euphoria" is also known for
its endless tracking shots

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:44.440
which give it a feeling
of constant attention

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.420
and surveillance, accumulating
tension with each moment.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:48.320
Which isn't al that different

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.920
from out feels to be a
teenager, painfully self-aware,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.550
worried that everyone's
watching and judging you.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.900
What's more, the colors are just as vivid

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:59.710
as the camera movements, a
bike ride to meet a crush takes

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.960
on a dreamy yellow palette that evokes

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.610
that ineffable sense
of being in a fairytale

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:06.400
the first time you fall in love.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.120
Pinkish hues express the overwhelming joy

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.250
of an intoxicated night out with friends.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.750
And of course, a dark
blue palette emphasizes

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:16.070
the utter depths of Rue's depression.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.590
Oftentimes in modern
cinema and television,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.450
shaky handheld camera movements

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:22.130
are used to evoke a point of view

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.600
and literally put you
in the character's shoes

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.030
in order to better
understand their experience.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.850
But "Euphoria" is highly stylized choices

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:30.830
do something different.

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:32.080
They evoke the characters

00:10:33.083 --> 00:10:34.460
outsized feelings about their experiences.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.360
As the creator, Sam Levinson explained

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.420
he eschewed cinematic realism

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.150
and instead stroke to make
each scene an interpretation

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:44.920
of reality or a representation
of an emotional reality.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:47.240
Each shot contributes
to an emotional sense

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:48.800
of what the character is feeling

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.980
rather than the actual movements
of their everyday life.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.534
Critics sometimes wonder why
"Euphoria" has to take place

00:10:54.534 --> 00:10:57.790
in high school, asking why
the characters couldn't just

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:58.930
be 20 somethings

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:00.854
with fully developed frontal cortex,

00:11:00.854 --> 00:11:03.064
a choice which would
probably render the show

00:11:03.064 --> 00:11:04.570
less controversial.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.020
But the emotional realism
is key to understanding

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.530
why "Euphoria" only makes
sense when set in high school.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.280
When you're a teenager,

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.080
all your emotions are
heightened to the nth degree.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:18.540
Love does feel like being
subsumed in a bath of pink light.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.640
Having a drink does make the world feel

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.670
like it flipped over.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.140
Well, we might not have
had the same experiences

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:27.090
as the way to experienced
"Euphoria" teens have.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:28.120
We can certainly relate

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.230
to the outsized feelings that
we're cinematically persuaded

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.200
to share with them.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:33.140
But we still

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:36.270
haven't fully explained
why empathy is so important

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:37.690
to understanding "Euphoria"

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:39.630
and it's perpetually troubled teens.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:40.930
To piece that together,

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:43.440
we need to look back to Rifkin who argues

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.900
that we are in an era of
dramaturgical consciousness.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.330
This means we take a more theatrical view

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:52.340
of life and our identities,
all the world's a stage

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.480
in a society that's ardently preoccupied

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.250
with social media and
subsumed by surveillance.

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:59.550
It's no surprise that
teens glued to their phones

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.470
construct their lives with
the attention to detail

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.300
of both audience and
director and those audiences

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:06.823
and the performances they expect to see

00:12:06.823 --> 00:12:09.390
change with every interaction.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.370
That means we essentially construct

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.410
and reconstruct our
ourselves from interaction

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.750
to interaction like how cam work brings

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.070
out a different side of
Kat as she plays the role

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.490
men seem to want.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.190
Now, role playing therapy

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:23.880
where you act out different personas

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.460
to better understand your
psychological hangups,

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.440
was common amongst the baby boomers

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.580
in their young adulthood.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:32.670
But Rifkin says, "Role
playing is no longer

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:33.920
a therapeutic technique,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.520
but rather a form of consciousness."

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.260
Like how you play someone different

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.230
on weird Twitter than
in an email to your mom.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:42.872
In our increasingly digital reality,

00:12:42.872 --> 00:12:45.560
we are all constantly reconstructing

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.280
and re understanding ourselves
in every interaction,

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.630
creating dispersed identities
that are more complex

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.800
and nuanced than the fixed
understanding people used to have

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:55.960
about human nature.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:59.000
We see this in the narrative
construction of "Euphoria."

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.296
It shows each character as complex

00:13:00.296 --> 00:13:03.840
and nuanced as they
negotiate their own identity.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.980
Sometimes this is
painful or uncomfortable.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:07.813
- Who am I?

00:13:07.813 --> 00:13:09.210
(laughs)

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.520
Oof, wow, wow, wow, wow, wow.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:15.370
- [Narrator] But by say,
showing even Nate's father

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.360
as a young love truck man,

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.220
we experience a different side of him.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:23.360
One that isn't as easy to
dismiss as a chili monster.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.900
This is the same way
understanding Rue's drug addiction

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:28.340
requires knowing how deeply
the loss of her father

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.510
still weighs on her.

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:32.300
Characters who might be
depicted as unsympathetic,

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.740
flat and static on another show
are thus given more context

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:38.270
to facilitate our
emotional concern for them.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:40.280
And "Euphoria's" sense of radical empathy

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:42.020
extends to every character.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:44.230
We'd argue that's a pretty cool thing.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:46.600
And plenty of people would agree with us.

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.470
In fact, more or less like
David Hume and Adam Smith

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.910
even argued that empathy is
a prerequisite for morality.

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:54.750
They used the word sympathy instead

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.760
but essentially described empathy.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:57.820
This makes sense.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.100
So much of morality is about
how you treat other people

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.350
and you can't treat people
well, unless you understand

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:07.520
and recognize their complexities,
subjectivity and humanity.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:08.939
And when we don't do that,

00:14:08.939 --> 00:14:11.900
it becomes much easier
to ignore systemic issues

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:13.850
like addiction, homelessness, disease

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.410
and even climate change.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:16.640
Empathy is about more

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.310
than isolated interactions
between individuals.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.580
It's about seeing the world ethically

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.440
and the people who populate that world

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.780
not as ideas but as complete human beings.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.000
Sometimes we even see characters learning

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:30.710
to do just that on "Euphoria."

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.340
Like when Rue decides not
to run away with Jules

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.960
because she's learned to empathize

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:37.400
with how much it might
her mother and sister.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:40.660
Along with Rue, the audience
is also developing empathy.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:43.779
And that's why watching the
show can feel so exhausting.

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:47.110
Empathy is hard work and
it's easier to judge people

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:50.540
based on abstractions than
to genuinely connect to them.

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:53.380
Especially when you're watching
people on the silver screen.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:54.910
Think about reality TV.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:56.610
When someone gets a villain at it

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.680
an empathetic reaction is about as likely

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:01.060
as a bachelor in
paradise, couple surviving

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:02.260
their third anniversary.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.020
Rue explicitly points out

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.310
the chronic lack of empathy in the world.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:09.563
- Everyone is just out to make
everyone else not seem human.

00:15:15.219 --> 00:15:17.210
I don't know if wanna be a part of it.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.940
- Rue's response as a
nihilistic kid is understandable

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:21.940
but the only way to surpass these kinds

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:23.890
of hasty judgments is empathy.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:26.280
And when we do choose to be a part of it,

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.730
it can be a rewarding and
uniquely modern experience.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:31.330
But what do you guys think?

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.260
Is "Euphoria" the most
compelling show on television,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:36.910
is my heart being broken
every week for no reason?

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:38.639
Let us know in the comments.

00:15:38.639 --> 00:15:41.200
Big thanks to our Patreons
for all your support

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.200
and be sure to check out our podcast.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.020
Hit that subscribe button

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:46.830
like you're desperately pausing the TV

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.290
when your parents walk in

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.300
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.920
And as always, thanks for watching, later.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.587
(upbeat music)

